  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 240 AM MST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nA closed low and modest 60-80 kt jet streak at 500-mb is forecast to \nbe just to the east of the forecast area going into Wednesday \nevening. Weak upper flow looks to replace this exiting trough in \nresponse to a weak low level baroclinic zone across the area. \nEnsembles are in support of this pattern persisting into Friday, \nwith primarily weak surface winds (below 15 MPH) and temperatures \nnear or a little below average. Lows Wednesday night are forecast \nmainly in the lower 20s with a few upper teens. Thursday highs look \nto be in the upper 40s to lower 50s, with lows in the upper 20s to \nlower 30s. Currently, no hazards are expected during this period. \n\nEnsembles are in support of the upper pattern becoming more \nnorthwesterly overhead by Friday morning as a shortwave trough digs \nacross the Midwest. This would be associated with a surface wind \nshift out of the northwest. Downsloping winds favor high \ntemperatures increasing Friday afternoon, with the current forecast \nin the upper 50s and lower 60s, and lows in the upper 20s and 30s. \nNBM guidance suggests that wind gusts with the front will primarily \nbe in the 20-30 MPH range, though the lower end of this range is \nfavored considering NBM overestimation biases. Fire weather is \ncurrently not a concern, as relative humidities (RH) are forecast to \nremain in the upper 20s and higher across the area. \n\nWhile deterministic guidance begins to diverge by Saturday night, \nensemble means seem to support northwesterly flow persisting at \nleast into Sunday evening, with a potentially more active weather \npattern to follow. There is a wide spread in high temperatures \nSaturday and Sunday, with a range of highs (10th to 90th percentile \nNBM) from the mid 40s to mid 60s. This could be associated with \nshortwaves embedded in the northwesterly flow that allow \ntemperatures to meander a bit, though guidance is currently unclear \non the type, timing, and intensity of shortwaves at this time. \nAdditionally, about 10-20% of LREF solutions show some light \nsnowfall occurring in portions of Eastern Colorado Saturday. This \nmay be in association with a 700-mb shortwave trough providing \nweak forcing in the region. Most solutions showing snowfall \nsuggest around an inch or less. Beyond Sunday, ensembles show \nseveral 500-mb shortwaves that could traverse the area, though \nguidance does not favor precipitation at this time. The range of\nhighs decreases a little bit Monday, but is still in the 40s \nand 50s.",
